Newspeak3
'Test'
(* newspeak-spec.pdf 3.1.1: "A value object is globally unique, in the
   sense that no other object is equal to it", and a value's class "does
   not define an identity method (==) ... if such a method is defined, that
   method must return the same results as Value's equality method (=)".
   `Value` declares `==` as a send of `=`, which makes the two agree by
   construction for every value class -- a value has no observable
   identity, so there is nothing else for `==` to answer.

   Each line prints the pair's `=` and then its `==`, in that order, so the
   agreement is visible column by column: the two are never allowed to
   differ.

   `Float/Integer` and `Integer/Float` answer false in both columns, and
   that is the rule holding rather than breaking: every value class's `=`
   is class-tight, `Float`'s included, so neither `1.0 = 1` nor `1 = 1.0`
   is true and `==` must answer exactly that. `Float>>=` widened an
   Integer argument until 2026-09-23, which made the two lines disagree
   and broke the `=`/`hash` contract with them: `1.0 hash` and `1 hash`
   differ. Cross-type numeric equality comes back with the numeric tower
   under `Number` (`docs/design/core-library.md`).

   `Text` is the case a user subclass of a builtin value class makes. Its
   instances carry no string payload, so `String>>=`'s leaf declines and
   the body's `super = other` reaches `Value`'s `=`; that leaf declines in
   turn -- the class's `=` is `String`'s, not `Value`'s -- and the Newspeak
   walk compares the classes and then zero slots. Two instances of a
   stateless value class are therefore one value: `true`, where identity
   answered `false` before `Value` declared anything. That is the reading
   3.1.1 requires -- a value with no state has nothing to tell two of them
   apart -- and it is recorded here because it is the one outcome of this
   change that no other golden shows.

   The module is a value (`Object <: Value`) because §3.1.1 asks a value
   class's enclosing object to be one; the transcript lives in `Script`,
   which is no value. *)
class ValueIdentity usingPlatform: platformAtCreation = Object <: Value () (
    public class Text = String new () ()
    class Script on: aTranscript = Object new (
        | transcript = aTranscript. |
    ) (
        report: label for: first and: second = (
            transcript show: label; show: ' '; show: (first = second) printString;
                show: ' '; show: (first == second) printString; newLine
        )
        public run = (
            report: 'String' for: 'a' and: 'a'.
            report: 'Symbol' for: #a and: #a.
            report: 'Integer' for: 3 and: 3.
            report: 'Nil' for: nil and: nil.
            report: 'Float/Integer' for: 1.0 and: 1.
            report: 'Integer/Float' for: 1 and: 1.0.
            report: 'Text' for: Text new and: Text new.
        )
    )
    public main: platform args: arguments = (
        (Script on: platform transcript) run
    )
) : ()
